{{!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  training/nav-button.hbs — the floating training-navigation button.

  Below lg the section rail has nowhere to live, so this is how it is opened.
  It replaces the hamburger that used to sit in the site header: that button
  made the SITE menu behave like a training control, so a reader tapping the
  most familiar icon on the page got a lesson list instead of the navigation
  they asked for. The header is the header again, and the training carries its
  own control.

  Bottom centre, because a lesson is read one-handed and that is where the
  thumb already is. Elevated, because it floats above the page rather than
  sitting in it — the one place a shadow is a description rather than a
  decoration in a system built on hairlines.

  It opens .ns-lesson-panel (the shared player sheet) via the `lessonNav`
  Alpine store — the same store the panel and the ✕ in its bar already use, so
  there is exactly one source of truth for "is the navigation open".

  The lesson count is filled in by training-progress.js. It is the reason to
  tap: "3/9" answers where am I before anything opens.
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ --}}
<div x-data x-cloak>
  <button type="button" class="ns-train-fab" x-show="!$store.lessonNav.open"
          @click="$store.lessonNav.open = true"
          :aria-expanded="$store.lessonNav.open.toString()"
          aria-controls="training-curriculum" aria-label="{{t "Training navigation"}}">
    <i class="ph-fill ph-list-checks" aria-hidden="true"></i>
    <span>{{t "Contents"}}</span>
    <span class="ns-train-fab__count js-train-count"></span>
  </button>
</div>
